A||||4||My7Fieros||How difficult is this?||||2||
Z||000000||My7Fieros||10-08-2000||09:12 PM||||I just read a post about someone inquiring about a dogbone. Mbramble was talking about also replacing his engine mounts and tranny mounts. How difficult is this to do? Ive never really thought about it, but if its not too difficult (I am pretty mechanically inclined, thanx to owning several Fieros) I'll probobly go on and do it. TIA, Brian||||reg||2
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-08-2000||09:20 PM||||Well if you go in for replacement of all the bushings.. they cost ~$165 (I think) and a few hundred for labor.  Probably not a hard job, but time consuming.  I had been quoted $500 for the complete job.||||reg||4
Z||000002||My7Fieros||10-08-2000||09:34 PM||||<B> D'OH!!! </B> Not exactly what I wanted to hear! Maybe it can wait then! ||||reg||11
Z||000003||Bob Englert||10-08-2000||09:37 PM||||Mount repalcement is fairly easy.  The Haynes manual has the basics.  You need to first remove the dogbone and loosen the nuts securing the mounts to the cradle.  Support the engine and transmission with a floor jack and remove the remaining fasteners. Be careful not to dent the oil pan, a block of wood works well.<P>YOu just need to force the engine and transmission up off the mounts enough to be able to remove and replace them.  <P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||10-09-2000||09:41 AM||||If you have a V6, don't forget to disconnect the rubber duct to the throddle body.  That would really get torqed around with it attached.||||reg||1
